# Independent Method Claim

**Claim 1.** A method for inserting an insulated inner pipe (10) into a jacket pipe (20), the method comprising:

providing an inner pipe (10) surrounded by an insulation layer (12), the insulation layer (12) having an outer diameter greater than an inner diameter of a jacket pipe (20);

inserting a leading end (14) of the inner pipe (10) and the surrounding insulation layer (12) into a first end (22) of the jacket pipe (20);

sealing the first end (22) of the jacket pipe (20) around the inner pipe (10) by means of a sealing device (30) to form a substantially airtight chamber (40) between an outer surface (16) of the insulation layer (12) and an inner surface (24) of the jacket pipe (20);

introducing a pressurized fluid (50) into the chamber (40) via a pressure inlet (32) provided in the sealing device (30), thereby generating an overpressure within the chamber (40) relative to an ambient pressure surrounding the insulation layer (12);

compressing the insulation layer (12) radially inward under the overpressure to temporarily reduce the outer diameter of the insulation layer (12) to a dimension smaller than the inner diameter of the jacket pipe (20);

axially displacing the inner pipe (10), together with the compressed insulation layer (12), through the jacket pipe (20) from the first end (22) toward a second end (26) of the jacket pipe (20) while maintaining the overpressure within the chamber (40); and

releasing the overpressure within the chamber (40) after the inner pipe (10) has been displaced to a predetermined position within the jacket pipe (20), thereby allowing the insulation layer (12) to radially expand and engage the inner surface (24) of the jacket pipe (20).

---

**Notes on claim structure** (for context, not part of the claim):
- Reference numerals are illustrative and would correspond to a set of accompanying drawings.
- Additional dependent claims could specify: the type of pressurized fluid (e.g., compressed air, inert gas), the sealing device configuration (e.g., inflatable seal, mechanical gland), monitoring/control of the overpressure (e.g., via a pressure sensor and controller), the insulation material (e.g., foamed polymer, mineral wool), or a pulling/pushing mechanism (e.g., winch, hydraulic ram) used to effect the axial displacement.

# PATENT CLAIM

**Claim 1:**

A method for producing a pre-insulated pipe assembly, the method comprising:

providing a service pipe having an outer surface and defining a longitudinal axis;

wrapping or disposing a compressible thermal insulation material circumferentially about the outer surface of the service pipe to form an insulated pipe core, the compressible insulation material having a first, uncompressed outer diameter;

forming at least one guide channel along a longitudinal length of the compressible insulation material, the at least one guide channel extending substantially parallel to the longitudinal axis and configured to receive a positioning element for maintaining concentric alignment of the service pipe relative to a surrounding jacket pipe during subsequent processing;

providing an outer jacket pipe having an inner diameter smaller than the first, uncompressed outer diameter of the compressible insulation material, such that the compressible insulation material must be radially compressed to a second, compressed outer diameter to permit insertion into the jacket pipe;

applying an axial pressure-assisted insertion force to the insulated pipe core sufficient to radially compress the compressible insulation material from the first outer diameter to the second outer diameter while simultaneously advancing the insulated pipe core longitudinally into the inner diameter of the jacket pipe;

maintaining the at least one guide channel in a substantially uncollapsed or partially collapsed state during the pressure-assisted insertion to preserve the concentric alignment of the service pipe within the jacket pipe;

wherein the radial compression of the compressible insulation material generates a residual outward radial force against an inner surface of the jacket pipe sufficient to substantially eliminate air gaps between the compressible insulation material and the inner surface of the jacket pipe; and

wherein, upon completion of the pressure-assisted insertion, the compressible insulation material at least partially rebounds within the at least one guide channel to further secure the service pipe in a substantially concentric position relative to the jacket pipe, thereby forming the pre-insulated pipe assembly.

---

**Claim 2 (dependent, optional addition):**

The method of claim 1, wherein the step of forming the at least one guide channel comprises longitudinally embedding a removable or permanent spacer strip within the compressible insulation material prior to the pressure-assisted insertion step.

**Claim 3 (dependent, optional addition):**

The method of claim 1, further comprising applying a lubricant to at least one of the outer surface of the compressible insulation material or the inner surface of the jacket pipe prior to the pressure-assisted insertion step to reduce frictional resistance during compression and advancement.
